# function starts(text: string, prefix: string) { return text.startsWith(prefix); }
# function ends(text: string, suffix: string) { return text.endsWith(suffix); }
# function finds(text: string, sought: string, from: number) { return <String.prototype.indexOf>.call(text, sought, from); }      sink(starts, ends, finds);
# The receiver is asked whether it is a text; of the other operands nothing is known, so the built-in is called with boxed operands: the stub looks at them and answers.
# The function itself is needed only where the stub cannot answer and leaves for the host's function. So the site hands over the number of the function's cell, one move,
# and the way out to the host reads the cell by that number. It was: a call of the stub BuiltinCell in front of every such call (TypeScript 6: 1.26 M entries).
